/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  letdown01_ammonia_flashing -- A LARGE PRESSURE LETDOWN, WITH THE ENGINE
  RUNNING (DEV.md 4c C64, the "Large pressure letdown" EduTool).

  THE QUESTION.  The liquid ammonia that leaves a synthesis loop's
  separator at 140 bar has to reach an atmospheric store.  That is a
  pressure ratio of about 140, and it is taken in two throttles, each
  followed by a drum that lets the released gas go.  What does the stream
  look like on the far side of each valve -- its temperature, how much of
  it is vapour, and how much SPACE it now occupies?  And what does the
  same throttle do to a gas -- the loop purge -- let down to a fuel header?

  WHERE THE STATES COME FROM.
    * RawLiquid (0/RawLiquid) -- the converged separator liquid of
      tutorials/plant/greenAmmonia03_basicDesign (converged/RawLiquid),
      copied to the digit: 140 bar, 253.15 K, 2927 kmol/h of ammonia
      carrying the H2, N2 and Ar the separator dissolved in it.
      gui/tests/letdown.test.ts holds the copy to the plant's own file.
    * the two pressures, 20 bar and 1.01325 bar -- the plant's own
      LetdownHP and StorageFlash drums
      (greenAmmonia03_basicDesign/LetdownHP/system/flowsheetDict,
      .../StorageFlash/system/flowsheetDict).
    * Purge (0/Purge) -- the same plant's converged purge gas (140 bar,
      253.15 K, mostly H2 and N2), copied to the digit.
    * 2 bar for the purge's fuel header -- AUTHOR-SET for the lesson.  The
      plant sends its purge to a boundary and models no letdown of it; no
      real fuel system is described by this number.

  WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM THE PLANT, AND WHY.  The plant takes each drop as
  an ISOTHERMAL flash whose temperature it declares (253.15 K at 20 bar,
  238.15 K at 1 atm) -- a hidden valve and a hidden heat exchanger in one
  box (see credo01_valve_heater_drum).  Here each drop is a `valve`, an
  isenthalpic throttle (src/unitOperations/valve/Valve.H): the outlet
  temperature and vapour fraction are RESULTS, and the drum after it has
  no knobs at all, inheriting T and P from the valve.  The plant's own
  StorageFlash header names the adiabatic letdown as the real one.

  WHAT THE VALVE PUBLISHES.  Besides T_out and vf, the valve's volumetric
  flow on both sides (Q_in, Q_out, volumeRatio, rho_in, rho_out), built
  from the package's own phase densities -- homogeneous, no slip.  The
  liquid density is the NH3 record's Vliq carried in T by Rackett, and the
  dissolved H2, N2 and Ar, which have no liquid density at 253 K, are
  priced at the density of the rest; the run says so.

  Package: the plant's own (Henry for the dissolved gases in ammonia, SRK
  for the vapour), copied with its case-local Ar-NH3 pair.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    //  The first throttle: separator liquid, 140 -> 20 bar.  The liquid
    //  left the separator in equilibrium with the loop gas at 140 bar, so
    //  even a small pressure reduction can begin to release dissolved gas.
    {
        name        ValveHP;
        type        valve;
        in          RawLiquid;
        outputs     ( ThrottledHP );
        operation
        {
            P       20 bar;
        }
    }
    {
        name        DrumHP;
        type        isothermalFlash;
        in          ThrottledHP;
        outputs     ( LetdownLiquid  LetdownGas );
        operation   { }             // NO knobs: T and P from ThrottledHP
    }
    //  The second throttle: 20 bar -> the atmospheric store.  Below the
    //  ammonia's own vapour pressure, the ammonia itself boils.
    {
        name        ValveLP;
        type        valve;
        in          LetdownLiquid;
        outputs     ( ThrottledLP );
        operation
        {
            P       1.01325 bar;
        }
    }
    {
        name        DrumLP;
        type        isothermalFlash;
        in          ThrottledLP;
        outputs     ( ProductNH3  FlashGas );
        operation   { }
    }
    //  The gas: the loop purge, 140 -> 2 bar.  A pressure ratio of 70 --
    //  far beyond the critical ratio of any gas.
    {
        name        PurgeValve;
        type        valve;
        in          Purge;
        outputs     ( FuelGas );
        operation
        {
            P       2 bar;          // author-set fuel header, see above
        }
    }
);
